<article class="env-lemma" id="07ZT">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07ZT">Lemma <span data-tag="07ZT">31.14.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $Z \subset S$ be a locally principal closed subscheme. Let $U = S \setminus Z$. Then $U \to S$ is an affine morphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The question is local on $S$, see Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/01S8" data-tag="01S8">29.11.3</a>. Thus we may assume $S = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ and $Z = V(f)$ for some $f \in A$. In this case $U = D(f) = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A_ f)$ is affine hence $U \to S$ is affine. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>